WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:06.799
Earlier this year, United Airlines made headlines,
signing a purchase agreement with Boom Supersonic

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:11.500
to buy 15 of their future supersonic jet airliners.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.110
In what could be a bold return of supersonic
travel, Boom is looking to develop the next

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.619
generation of supersonic planes.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:24.730
Building on the legacy of the iconic Concorde,
and attempting to address the problems that

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:30.029
led to the Concorde's final flight on October
24th 2006.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:32.970
The Concorde was a marvel of engineering.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.290
Capable of flying from London to New York
in under 3 hours.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:42.740
So fast that passengers, thanks to the 5 hour
time zone difference, had to rewind their

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.980
clocks two hours on arrival, arriving earlier
than they left.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:53.640
Flying across the Atlantic in the Concorde
was super easy, barely an inconvenience, so

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.790
why did it stop being produced?

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:59.210
The Concorde failed for several reasons.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:04.800
The Concorde had over 100 purchase agreements
with major airlines of the day, including

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.270
United Airlines, but soaring production costs
forced everyone but British Airways and Air

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.460
France to cancel their orders.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.400
Then, cramped and limited seating along with
rising fuel prices made turning a profit for

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.460
British Airways and Air France difficult.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.030
Causing ticket prices as high as eleven thousand
dollars.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:31.070
Why fly on the cramped Concorde at that price
when you could purchase a first class reclining

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:35.470
seat that only took 4 hours longer at a fraction
of the price?

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:41.409
At that price, the Concorde was always going
to struggle to fill seats, and that just got

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:47.870
harder after the unfortunate air crash of
Air France 4560 in 2000.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:53.500
These economic problems were exacerbated further
by the extremely limited routes available

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.500
to the Concorde.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.480
Supersonic travel was banned over the United
States and Europe, excluding the plane from

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.760
some of the world’s most valuable airline
routes.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.189
The plane’s range was also limited, preventing
it from capitalizing on potentially lucrative

00:02:09.189 --> 00:02:11.099
transpacific routes.

00:02:11.099 --> 00:02:16.980
In it’s 27 years of operation, the Concordes
only regularly scheduled routes were across

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.329
the Atlantic between New York, and London
and Paris.

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:26.989
In order for the next generation of supersonic
planes to succeed, they need to address these

00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:31.739
problems, and today we are going to see how
Boom is looking to prevail where Concorde

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:33.099
couldn’t.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:35.939
First off, those pesky sonic booms.

00:02:35.939 --> 00:02:40.150
Anything travelling over the speeds of sound
is going to cause a sonic boom.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.269
That’s just a fact of nature.

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:48.549
This became a major issue in the 1960s, when
hearing sonic booms was not a terribly rare

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:49.810
occurrence.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:55.299
With Cold War fear at an all time high, the
Air Force regularly conducted military drills

00:02:55.299 --> 00:03:02.439
near cities, and in the fall of 1965, public
acceptance of this nuisance was becoming a

00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:08.900
serious political issue, forcing the US Government
to form the National Sonic Boom Evaluation

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.049
Office.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:15.249
This group then began taking measurements
of sonic booms with different planes, atmospheric

00:03:15.249 --> 00:03:20.040
conditions, and flight altitudes to gauge
public acceptance.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.950
They recorded boom signatures with pressure
gauges and created these sonic boom signatures.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:32.720
Here’s three signatures, one for the F-104,
one for the B-58 and one for the XB-70.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.121
We see a peak in pressure caused by the sonic
boom followed by a drop in pressure, and a

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:42.799
final jump in pressure caused by the tail
of the aircraft.

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:48.779
Interestingly we can see that the signature
for the XB-70 is longer than the others, and

00:03:48.779 --> 00:03:51.079
that’s because the plane itself is longer.

00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:52.079
[3]

00:03:52.079 --> 00:03:56.949
The studies uncovered a huge amount about
how supersonic waves travel through the atmosphere,

00:03:56.949 --> 00:04:02.799
and honestly the entire study needs a video
in itself, because it’s incredibly complicated

00:04:02.799 --> 00:04:04.169
and rich in history.

00:04:04.169 --> 00:04:11.030
But for now, the important thing is that people
absolutely hated these sonic booms.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:19.209
The key word that emerged from the 6,375 interviews
conducted was “startling” and people did

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.669
not get used to them; the annoyance only got
worse over time.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:28.060
Many complained about physical damage to their
house as the pressure waves could physically

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.699
rattle windows.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:36.139
NASA is planning to revisit these tests in
2022 with the X-59 which has been designed

00:04:36.139 --> 00:04:42.150
with decades of research to minimize the pressure
associated with sonic booms.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:49.009
With highly swept delta wings, a blunt duck-billed
nose and a very narrow fuselage that narrows

00:04:49.009 --> 00:04:52.569
over the wings to conform to the area rule
[4].

00:04:52.569 --> 00:04:58.190
With these design considerations the X-59
is expected to have a boom with a perceived

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:00.340
decibel level of about 75.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.479
[5] Which the NASA team equates to the sound
of distant thunder.

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:12.300
They plan to begin testing public acceptance
of this again in 2022, but critically Overture,

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:19.050
Boom’s concept commercial plane, features
few if any of these distinctive low boom designs

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:24.460
and will likely have a boom just as loud as
the Concorde which registered at a perceived

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.550
decibel level of 105.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:33.669
That blunt nose increases drag and reduces
fuel efficiency, while a narrowing fuselage

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:35.570
reduces space for passengers.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:42.240
Sacrificing range and passenger capacity for
the sake of quiter sonic booms is not an option

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.639
for a commercial transport plane.

00:05:44.639 --> 00:05:49.710
And judging by the routes advertised on Boom’s
website, this is a problem they are perfectly

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:56.360
aware of and are instead focusing on increasing
range and opening up those valuable transpacific

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:57.360
routes.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.389
So, how are they going to manage that?

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:05.570
Thankfully the airline industry has been heavily
focused on increasing fuel efficiency and

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:11.879
range in the past 5 decades since the Concorde
was designed, and the team at Boom will be

00:06:11.879 --> 00:06:15.220
able to take advantage of many of these advancements.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.729
The Concorde was primarily constructed from
Aluminium.

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:23.389
Overture will primarily be constructed from
carbon fibre composites, similar to the Boeing

00:06:23.389 --> 00:06:29.539
787, which allowed Boeing to reduce weight
and drag while creating extremely thin and

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:31.590
perfectly sculpted wings.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:36.990
Bending aluminium into the perfect shape without
creating stress concentrations is difficult,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:40.409
with composites we have much more freedom
in design.

00:06:40.409 --> 00:06:45.090
Carbon Fibre also comes with the advantage
of lower thermal expansion.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:51.860
The aluminium structure of the Concorde expanded
by 0.3 metres at cruise, which had to be accounted

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:57.020
for with expansion joints in the fuselage
and hydraulic piping and slack in critical

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.349
wiring

00:06:58.349 --> 00:07:03.039
The Overture for the most part shares a very
similar design to the Concorde.

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:08.620
A design that focused on minimizing supersonic
drag, while being capable of providing enough

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.470
lift at low speed flight for take-offs and
landings.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:16.930
Let’s compare the top view of both the Concorde
and the Overture.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:22.370
The first similarity we can immediately spot
are the ogival delta wings.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:27.509
This is a form of compound delta wing where
we have two sections to the wing, with the

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:31.949
forward section having a higher sweep angle
than the rear section.

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:37.880
The ogival delta wing of the Concorde is a
variation of the compound delta, with an ogee

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.409
curve connecting each section.

00:07:40.409 --> 00:07:44.030
Going from concave to convex curves smoothly.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:50.090
This shape was used to optimize for both low
speed subsonic flight and high speed supersonic

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:51.310
flight.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:56.719
Creating a wing that can minimize drag at
supersonic speeds was vital, but these short

00:07:56.719 --> 00:08:02.790
wingspan wings create issues when taking off
or landing, as the plane cannot generate enough

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.749
lift at slow speeds.

00:08:04.749 --> 00:08:11.130
The ogival delta wing of the Concorde helped
alleviate this by generating vortex lift.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:16.999
Where at high angles of attack, separated
air flow would roll over the wings to form

00:08:16.999 --> 00:08:23.280
two stable cone shaped vortices where air
speeds were high and air pressure was low.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.470
Effectively creating lift.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:30.159
This is why we see the Concorde landing with
its nose extremely high, which would have

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:33.760
completely blinded the pilot's view of the
ground.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.570
The engineers of the Concorde solved this
by creating a nose that could actually angle

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:43.420
downwards on command, to allow the pilots
to see the runways when landing.

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:48.900
The mechanics of this system of course increased
the weight of the plane, required routine

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:53.710
maintenance, increased operating costs and
the mechanism itself would have increased

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:56.330
the overall price of the aircraft.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:02.850
The Overture will not feature this droop snoot,
but instead use a system of cameras and screens

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.380
to allow the pilots to keep their eyes on
the runway even when their cockpit windows

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.450
show nothing but sky.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:14.690
The engineers at boom have a massive leg up
in engineering tools over their counterparts

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:19.640
at concorde, who would have had to design
the concorde with pencils and paper while

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.680
testing it in physical wind tunnels.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:28.920
The computational tools we have today are
absolutely mind blowingly advanced.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:34.730
Just take a look at this simulation performed
by NASA supercomputers modelling airflow around

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:36.940
the landing gear of a plane.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:42.490
This particular simulation was done to investigate
ways to improve noise generated from landing

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:48.600
gear and we can see this massive vortice rolling
off the landing gear doors.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:54.380
Telling us that we can improve noise generated
by focusing on airflow around these doors.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:59.750
In the past engineers would have had to use
low resolution physical visualisation techniques

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:05.970
to piece together ways to reduce noise and
create model after model to test different

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.040
designs.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:12.330
Today we can make the models in a computer
and test them on a computer, allowing incredibly

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:17.120
fast turn over times and vastly improved quantitative
measurements.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:22.350
Tools like this will be used by Boom to land
on the optimized airframe and carbon composites

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:26.880
will allow them to manufacture those optimized
shapes with ease.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:31.560
On the surface, the wings of the overture
are remarkably similar to the Concordes by

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:36.970
all accounts, which makes sense, they worked
very well, but in order to increase range

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:40.350
they can’t just be as good, they need to
be better.

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:44.750
It’s clear that Overture have made efforts
to make the wings thinner while extending

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:50.620
the delta wing further up the aircraft, seamlessly
integrating into the plane's sharp nose.

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:53.340
This will all help minimize wave drag.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.870
Wave drag occurs as shock waves form over
the plane.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:02.440
These shock waves act like huge invisible
air brakes, slowing down passing air significantly

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:04.760
and increasing drag.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:08.050
Area ruling is one of the key methods to reduce
it.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:12.340
The area rule is a weirdly simple rule in
aerodynamics.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:17.510
It tells us that the ideal distribution of
cross-sectional area along the length of a

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:20.610
supersonic plane should look something like
this.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:25.120
That includes the fuselage, wings, engines
and every other part of the plane.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:30.750
That’s why we often see waisted fuselages
that narrow to compensate for the increase

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:34.670
in cross sectional area caused by the wings.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:37.960
Planes never conform perfectly to the area
rule.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:43.290
We need wings and engines to fly, and narrowing
the fuselage over the wings isn’t really

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:47.660
an option for a commercial airliner that needs
to carry people.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:52.900
However, carbon fibre composites do give designers
much more freedom in shaping the aircraft

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:58.450
to conform to the area rule, and Overture
will likely benefit from these advances.

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:02.320
Overture will benefit from other advances
in material science that have enabled the

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.010
creation of incredibly efficient engines like
the GEnX.

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:12.480
Advances in aerodynamic modeling, metallurgy
and 3D printing have made today's jet engines

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:17.780
vastly more efficient than the engines of
the the 1960s and 70s, but it’s important

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:23.920
to note one major advance that supersonic
planes cannot take advantage of, high bypass

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:25.250
ratios.

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:30.380
Bypass ratios have continually grown over
the past 5 decades and this has been one of,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:36.520
if not the primary driver for increased efficiency,
and supersonic planes cannot take advantage

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:37.520
of it.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:38.520
[1a]

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:42.640
Supersonic jet engines need to be pure jets,
avoiding the high bypass ratios

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.550
of traditional airliners.

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:49.210
The large diameter of these engines would
cause an immense amount of drag, and the large

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:55.210
quantity of relatively slow moving air coming
from the bypass would actually just slow a

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.400
supersonic jet down.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:03.450
Instead the engines of the Concorde, and Booms
test planes use pure jets, with no bypass.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:08.680
These engines have not benefited from the
past 5 decades of research that high bypass

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:14.340
ratio planes have, and for Boom to succeed
they are going to have to develop an entirely

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:15.500
new engine.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:21.050
Boom’s flight demonstrator aircraft, the
XB-1, which is set to begin testing in 2022,

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:27.340
is using the GE J85, an engine that has been
in use since the 1950s.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.680
This simply will not do for the final passenger
aircraft,

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:36.380
and this year Boom announced a collaboration
with Rolls Royce to develop a propulsion system

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.390
for use with their aircraft.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:39.390
[8]

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:44.580
Engine development takes years if not decades
of development, so it will be interesting

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:51.280
to see what Rolls Royce can come up with before
Boom’s advertised first flight date of 2026.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:56.480
It should be noted that the primary way Overture
is going to reduce fuel consumption is by

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:02.280
slowing down.Overture is aiming for a cruise
speed of 1.7 mach [9], whereas the Concorde

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.240
cruised at mach 2.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:09.820
Even with all of these potential fuel saving
technologies Boom rather confusingly advertises

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:18.240
the range of the Overture at 7866 kilometres,
just over 500 kilometres over Concordes.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:24.170
One of the shorter routes over the Pacific
is Tokyo to Seattle at 7651 kilometres.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:29.810
I’m not sure whether Boom is factoring in
the additional fuel capacity required by regulators

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:34.630
to ensure planes have enough in reserve to
deal with emergencies, but this is cutting

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:39.490
things extremely close for even the shortest
pacific route,

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:44.030
and it most certainly is not enough for the
Sydney to Los Angeles flight advertised on

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.030
Boom’s website.

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:50.430
There is no doubt in my mind that we can build
a more efficient supersonic passenger jet

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:55.850
today, but I am skeptical we can build one
that will succeed economically.

00:14:55.850 --> 00:15:01.240
Boom appears to be trying very hard to obfuscate
the limitations of supersonic travel, and

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:06.610
are even advertising net zero carbon nonsense
about the plane being able to use sustainable

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:12.420
biofuel, which is not only not commercially
available at scale, but is vastly more expensive

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:13.850
than kerosene.

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:18.660
No flight operator is going to use biofuel
on a plane that will already be struggling

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.450
with ticket prices.

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:26.580
The average price of a round trip ticket from
London to New York was 12,000 dollars.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:31.800
It exclusively catered to the ultra-wealthy
who were happy to pay an insane premium to

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.780
save 2 hours of their day.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.330
A narrow market.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:40.780
A product most of us will only ever consider
as a once in a lifetime novelty, and that’s

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:44.090
not a sustainable business model for airlines.

00:15:44.090 --> 00:15:49.680
Without a doubt Boom can build a prototype
worthy of being the Concorde’s successor,

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:54.830
that improves on all aspects of supersonic
passenger travel, but whether they can build

00:15:54.830 --> 00:16:00.010
it at an affordable price point and get fuel
consumption down low enough for it to be commercially

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:06.210
viable is another challenge entirely, one
I’m not confident they can overcome.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:09.670
Overcoming challenges to me is the joy of
engineering.

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:15.080
That dopamine rush of solving a problem you
have been working on for days if not weeks.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:19.910
That feeling is what draws most engineers
into their career, and perhaps you have someone

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:24.970
in your family that gets that same joy, and
